Imports:
  - Types:
      - PrettyConfig
    From: prettyplay/config
  - Types:
      - StepReporter
    Usages:
      - hooks
    From: prettyplay/reporting

Usages:
  conventions: .goga/usages/conventions.md

Annotations: |
  Use `conventions` for code writing rules and testing.
  Use `hooks` from Imports for the payload contract of the cache events.

  The cache is a repository artifact: one .py file per step, addressed deterministically; the cache is always read, writes are best-effort.
  Step identity is intentional: the same normalized sentence in the same context is one step; a different language, a different step type or a different cache key is a different step.
  Step identity is built from the addressing text — the rendered sentence with interpolations substituted and active capture tags verbatim; a plain sentence addresses as itself; exact-text equality unifies a plain sentence and a template producing the same text. One verbatim normalization serves every addressing text: case and internal whitespace are significant, because rendered values are data.
  The cache is a per-test working set by default: the store accumulates the addresses it successfully loaded or saved, and the close-time sweep deletes the untouched files of its cache key in its scope — best-effort, logged, never raising.
  RunBudgets lives in this cell because its accounting key is `StepIdentity`: per-test attempt accounting stays next to the addressing it is keyed by; the recovery-cycle accounting joins the registry — per-group cycle counters keyed by the group key, beside the per-step pools. Budgets follow the addressing: a re-rendered identity draws a fresh budget.

---

"normalize_step_text(text: str) -> normalized: str":
  location: text.py
  annotations: |
    Normalize an addressing text for identity and addressing — one verbatim rule for every text.

    `text`: the addressing text of the step — the rendered sentence; a plain sentence addresses as itself.
    `normalized`: the normalized addressing text.

    Algorithm:
    1. Apply Unicode NFC normalization
    2. Trim leading and trailing whitespace — nothing else

    Requirements:
    - Pure function: no I/O, no locale dependence, deterministic on `text` alone
    - Case and internal whitespace are significant: «Click Sign in» and «click  sign in » are different steps — rendered values are data
    - Exact-text equality unifies: a plain sentence and a template producing the exact same text share one address

    Constraints:
    - No casefold, no internal whitespace collapse, no template-marker detection

"StepIdentity(cache_key: str, step_type: str, normalized_text: str)":
  location: models.py
  annotations: |
    The address of a cache step: the triple (cache_key, step_type, normalized_text) plus the deterministic file name derived from it.

    `cache_key`: the explicit context key set by the integrator on the main object.
    `step_type`: action or assertion.
    `normalized_text`: the step's addressing text after `normalize_step_text` — the rendered sentence, verbatim-normalized.
  properties:
    "cache_key -> str": |
      The explicit context key of the step address.
    "step_type -> str": |
      The step kind: action or assertion.
    "normalized_text -> str": |
      The normalized addressing text of the step — the rendered sentence after NFC + trim.
    "filename -> str": |
      The deterministic cache file name.

      Algorithm:
      1. Build the identity string: cache_key, step_type and normalized_text joined with an unambiguous separator
      2. Hash the string with sha256
      3. Compose the file name from the hex digest

      Requirements:
      - The same triple always yields the same file name; any difference in the triple yields a different one
      - The digest identifies the file unambiguously inside the cache directory

"CachedStep(identity: StepIdentity, addressing_text: str, code: str, created_at: str)":
  location: models.py
  annotations: |
    One cached step in memory: the metadata and the generated code of the step, as stored in its cache file.

    `identity`: the step address.
    `addressing_text`: the step's addressing text — the verbatim rendered sentence before NFC and trim; the metadata sentence field carries it.
    `code`: the generated step code — top-level imports when present, then the fixed-form def step function.
    `created_at`: the creation date.

    Requirements:
    - The cache file is a valid Python module: metadata fields first, then the sentinel line, then the step code; importing the module and reading the fields reconstructs the step
    - The file carries no library version field and is never invalidated by a library upgrade
    - The metadata sentence field carries the step's addressing text — the rendered sentence with actual values; rendered values on disk are accepted by the design, the security posture is a deferred question
    - normalize_step_text(addressing_text) == identity.normalized_text for every newly built cached step; the load path may restore legacy files whose STEP_TEXT already contains the normalized sentence
  properties:
    "identity -> StepIdentity": |
      The step address.
    "addressing_text -> str": |
      The step's addressing text — the verbatim rendered sentence before NFC and trim.
    "code -> str": |
      The generated step code — top-level imports when present, then the fixed-form def step function; stored and restored verbatim.
    "created_at -> str": |
      The creation date.

"StepCache(config: PrettyConfig, path: str | None, reporter: StepReporter | None)":
  location: store.py
  annotations: |
    The repository store of cache steps: addressing, atomic writes, the read-only mode — and the per-test working-set lifecycle: the touched-address accumulation and the close-time sweep.

    `config`: project settings; the cache_root setting is the cache root.
    `path`: the optional subdirectory inside the cache; part of the address — steps of different subdirectories never collide; empty — the shared root, so equal cache keys are reused across tests.
    `reporter`: the visibility point — cache events (saved, skipped) go through it; omitted — the hook-less default reporter, so a missing visibility point never fails a save.

    Requirements:
    - A successful load or save records the step's file name in the touched set — the object's working set; a miss, a damaged entry, a skipped or a failed write touch nothing
    - The touched set lives in the memory of the running process, one per StepCache instance — it never persists
  properties:
    "root -> str": |
      The effective cache root; the default is <cwd>/.prettyplay/cache/ — the working directory of the run.
    "writable -> bool": |
      Whether the cache directory accepts writes.
  methods:
    "load(identity: StepIdentity) -> step: CachedStep | None": |
      Load the cached step by address.

      Algorithm:
      1. Resolve the target file by the effective root, the subdirectory and the identity file name
      2. A missing file returns None
      3. Read the module, validate the metadata fields — normalize the STEP_TEXT value through `normalize_step_text` and compare it with the normalized_text of `identity`; restore that value as the addressing_text of the reconstructed `CachedStep` so the saved metadata is preserved — the code tail starts at the sentinel line — legacy files without it carry the code starting at def step(
      4. A successful reconstruct records the identity file name in the touched set; a miss or a damaged entry records nothing
    "save(step: CachedStep) -> stored: bool": |
      Store the step atomically, best-effort.

      `stored`: whether the step was stored — True — the atomic replace landed; False — the write was skipped (a read-only cache or a busy target; on_cache_skipped carries the reason).

      Algorithm:
      1. Check writability: a read-only cache skips the write and reports on_cache_skipped with the reason read-only cache
      2. Serialize the step into the module text: metadata fields — STEP_TEXT serialized from the addressing_text of `step` — then the sentinel line, then the code; the file name derives from the identity of `step` as before
      3. Write a temporary file with a unique name in the target directory
      4. Replace the target file atomically via os.replace
      5. On Windows, when the target is busy: retry the replace shortly, then skip the write for this step and report on_cache_skipped — the run does not fail
      6. Report on_cache_saved with the file name on success; a stored write records the file name in the touched set, a skipped or failed one records nothing

      Requirements:
      - Concurrent writers on one step never corrupt the file: last writer wins
      - A partially written file never becomes visible: the replace is atomic
      - The cache is always read, in every environment
    "sweep_untouched(cache_key: str) -> removed: list[str]": |
      Delete the untouched step files of one cache key in this store's scope — the per-test working-set cleanup.

      `cache_key`: the context key of the owning test object — the sweep considers only files whose metadata carries it.
      `removed`: the file names deleted by this call.

      Algorithm:
      1. The scope is the effective root plus the object's subdirectory
      2. Enumerate every step file in the scope
      3. Read the metadata fields: unreadable metadata — log a WARNING and skip the file, its ownership is unprovable in a possibly shared root; a cache_key other than `cache_key` — skip silently
      4. A file name present in the touched set survives; every other file of this key is deleted, one log record per deletion naming the file
      5. A per-file failure — reading or deleting — logs a WARNING and continues with the next file

      Requirements:
      - Never raises: the sweep is best-effort hygiene and must never fail the closing test
      - Idempotent: a repeated call deletes nothing new — the survivors are touched files, other keys' files and unreadable ones
      - Untouched legacy files of this key are swept by the first call after the upgrade
      - The method knows nothing of the cleanup switch — the caller decides whether to sweep

      Constraints:
      - Deletions are log records only — no hook event, a deferred contract decision
      - No address recomputation: the file name is the address — the sha256 of the triple; the touched set and the directory names share one key space

"RunBudgets(generation_limit: int, healing_limit: int)":
  location: budgets.py
  annotations: |
    The per-test attempt registry: how many generation and healing attempts each step has left within the test — and how many recovery cycles each group has left.

    `generation_limit`: the generation attempt budget per step per test.
    `healing_limit`: the healing attempt budget per step per test.

    Requirements:
    - The registry lives for the lifetime of one test, owned by the test's runtime: every test starts with full limits — a step reused across tests gets a fresh budget in each test
    - The identity key is `StepIdentity`; budgets follow the addressing — a step re-rendered against changed memory is a different identity and draws a fresh budget
    - An exhausted budget returns False — the caller turns it into the incurable failure
    - Ordinary per-step healing keeps its per-test pools exactly as today — the recovery semantics ride beside them
    - The recovery cycle cap of a group is the healing_limit value — the loop is never infinite

    Constraints:
    - No persistence: budgets exist only in the memory of the running process
  methods:
    "try_generation(identity: StepIdentity) -> allowed: bool": |
      Consume one generation attempt for the step; False — the budget is exhausted, the caller reports incurability.
    "try_healing(identity: StepIdentity) -> allowed: bool": |
      Consume one healing attempt for the step; False — the budget is exhausted, the caller reports incurability.
    "refresh_healing(identity: StepIdentity)": |
      Grant the step a fresh full healing counter — the recovery engine calls it at every new
      recovery cycle for every row step, so a second attempt is never starved by the first cycle.

      `identity`: the address of the row step.

      Requirements:
      - The next try_healing calls of the step draw from a renewed pool of the full healing_limit
    "open_group_cycle(group_key: str) -> allowed: bool": |
      Consume one recovery cycle for the group identified by `group_key` within this test.

      `group_key`: the key of the group — its group prompt.
      `allowed`: True — a new cycle is open; False — the per-group cycle cap, the healing_limit
      value, is exhausted — the caller turns it into the terminal incurable failure.

      Requirements:
      - The cap counts recovery cycles per group per test, never per step

